@page "/split-text"

<PageTitle>Split text - Bit.Bmotion</PageTitle>

<h1>Split text</h1>

<p class="page-intro">
    The headline that assembles itself one letter at a time is the oldest trick in motion design,
    and everywhere else it means cutting a sentence into spans with a script and hoping nothing
    re-renders underneath it. <strong>BmotionSplitText</strong> does the splitting in C#: each
    character, word or line becomes a real animated element in the render tree, so there is nothing
    to re-split, nothing to undo, and the text still wraps, selects and copies like the original.
</p>

<div class="demo-section">
    <h2>Characters, words, lines</h2>
    <p>
        One parameter picks the unit. <code>By="BmSplitBy.Chars"</code> is the headline treatment;
        <code>Words</code> keeps the count sane on longer copy; <code>Lines</code> splits on the
        newlines you authored, which is what you want for a stanza or an address block. Every unit
        gets the same <code>Initial</code> / <code>Animate</code> pair, offset by the stagger.
    </p>

    <div class="demo-row">
        <div class="demo-card stack">
            <span class="demo-label">By: Chars</span>
            <BmotionSplitText @key="_runId" Class="split-headline"
                              Text="Every character"
                              By="BmSplitBy.Chars"
                              Initial="Bm.To(opacity: 0, y: 24, rotate: -12)"
                              Animate="Bm.To(opacity: 1, y: 0, rotate: 0)"
                              Stagger="Bm.Stagger(0.03)"
                              Transition="Bm.Spring(bounce: 0.35, duration: 0.7)" />
        </div>

        <div class="demo-card stack">
            <span class="demo-label">By: Words</span>
            <BmotionSplitText @key="_runId" Class="split-headline"
                              Text="One word at a time"
                              By="BmSplitBy.Words"
                              Initial="Bm.To(opacity: 0, y: 20)"
                              Animate="Bm.To(opacity: 1, y: 0)"
                              Stagger="Bm.Stagger(0.08)"
                              Transition="Bm.Spring(bounce: 0.25, duration: 0.6)" />
        </div>

        <div class="demo-card stack">
            <span class="demo-label">By: Lines</span>
            <BmotionSplitText @key="_runId" Class="split-lines"
                              Text="@_stanza"
                              By="BmSplitBy.Lines"
                              Initial='Bm.To(opacity: 0, x: -30, filter: "blur(6px)")'
                              Animate='Bm.To(opacity: 1, x: 0, filter: "blur(0px)")'
                              Stagger="Bm.Stagger(0.12)"
                              Transition="Bm.Tween(0.6, BmEase.Out)" />
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="demo-actions">
        <button class="btn-bm" @onclick="Replay">Replay</button>
    </div>

    <CodeSnippet Code="@_basicsCode" />
</div>

<div class="demo-section">
    <h2>Where the cascade starts</h2>
    <p>
        The stagger is a <code>Bm.Stagger</code> like any other, so the same origins apply:
        <code>First</code> runs left to right, <code>Last</code> right to left, and
        <code>Center</code> opens outwards from the middle of the line. A custom
        <code>(index, total)</code> function covers everything else - the last stage below reverses
        every other character by hand.
    </p>

    <div class="demo-row">
        @foreach (var mode in _staggerModes)
        {
            <div class="demo-card stack">
                <span class="demo-label">@mode.Label</span>
                <BmotionSplitText @key="@($"{_runId}-{mode.Label}")" Class="split-headline"
                                  Text="Cascade"
                                  Initial="Bm.To(opacity: 0, scale: 0.4, y: 18)"
                                  Animate="Bm.To(opacity: 1, scale: 1, y: 0)"
                                  Stagger="mode.Stagger"
                                  Transition="Bm.Spring(bounce: 0.4, duration: 0.6)" />
            </div>
        }
    </div>

    <div class="demo-actions">
        <button class="btn-bm" @onclick="Replay">Replay</button>
    </div>

    <CodeSnippet Code="@_staggerCode" />
</div>

<div class="demo-section">
    <h2>On scroll, and under the pointer</h2>
    <p>
        Because every unit is a full <code>Bmotion</code> element, it takes the gesture parameters
        too. <code>WhileInView</code> with <code>Once</code> gives the classic reveal-as-you-scroll
        headline; <code>WhileHover</code> lands on the individual character the pointer is actually
        over, which is a per-letter interaction you would otherwise have to wire up by hand.
    </p>

    <div class="demo-row">
        <div class="demo-card stack">
            <span class="demo-label">WhileHover &middot; hover a letter</span>
            <BmotionSplitText Class="split-headline"
                              Text="Hover me"
                              WhileHover='Bm.To(y: -14, scale: 1.25, color: "#FD7F36")'
                              Transition="Bm.Spring(stiffness: 400, damping: 18)" />
        </div>

        <div class="demo-card stack">
            <span class="demo-label">WhileInView + Once &middot; scroll it into view</span>
            <BmotionSplitText Class="split-headline"
                              Text="Reveal on scroll"
                              By="BmSplitBy.Words"
                              Initial="Bm.To(opacity: 0, y: 28)"
                              WhileInView="Bm.To(opacity: 1, y: 0)"
                              Once="true"
                              Stagger="Bm.Stagger(0.08)"
                              Transition="Bm.Spring(bounce: 0.3, duration: 0.7)" />
        </div>
    </div>

    <CodeSnippet Code="@_gestureCode" />
</div>

<div class="demo-section">
    <h2>It still reads as a sentence</h2>
    <p>
        Splitting text is the classic way to break it for everyone who is not looking at it: a
        screen reader handed forty one-character spans spells the headline out. By default the
        container carries the whole string as its <code>aria-label</code> and every unit is
        <code>aria-hidden</code>, so assistive technology reads the sentence once. Whitespace is
        rendered as whitespace rather than as units, and in <code>Chars</code> mode each word is
        wrapped in an inline-block of its own - so the text wraps between words, never mid-word,
        and selecting it copies the original string back.
    </p>

    <div class="demo-row">
        <div class="demo-card stack">
            <span class="demo-label">Narrow container &middot; still wraps between words</span>
            <div class="split-narrow">
                <BmotionSplitText @key="_runId" Class="split-body"
                                  Text="Long copy still wraps between words, never inside one."
                                  Initial="Bm.To(opacity: 0, y: 10)"
                                  Animate="Bm.To(opacity: 1, y: 0)"
                                  Stagger="Bm.Stagger(0.012)"
                                  Transition="Bm.Tween(0.4)" />
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="demo-actions">
        <button class="btn-bm" @onclick="Replay">Replay</button>
    </div>

    <CodeSnippet Code="@_a11yCode" />
</div>

@code {
    // Every sample keys off this counter so "Replay" remounts them and their enter animations run
    // again - the same trick the presence pages use, rather than a bespoke reset path per demo.
    private int _runId;

    private void Replay() => _runId++;

    private const string _stanza = """
        Split on the newlines
        you actually wrote,
        not on the ones
        the browser happened to pick.
        """;

    private sealed record StaggerMode(string Label, BmStagger Stagger);

    private readonly StaggerMode[] _staggerModes =
    [
        new("from: First", Bm.Stagger(0.05)),
        new("from: Center", Bm.Stagger(0.05, from: BmStaggerFrom.Center)),
        new("from: Last", Bm.Stagger(0.05, from: BmStaggerFrom.Last)),
        new("custom (i, total)", Bm.Stagger((i, total) => (i % 2 == 0 ? i : total - i) * 0.05)),
    ];

    @* Four quotes: the snippet itself contains a triple-quoted raw string literal. *@
    private const string _basicsCode = """"
        @using Bit.Bmotion

        <style>
            .split-headline { font-size: 1.6rem; font-weight: 700; }
            .split-lines { font-size: 1rem; line-height: 1.6; }
        </style>

        @* Characters: the headline treatment. Each letter is its own animated element. *@
        <BmotionSplitText Class="split-headline"
                          Text="Every character"
                          By="BmSplitBy.Chars"
                          Initial="Bm.To(opacity: 0, y: 24, rotate: -12)"
                          Animate="Bm.To(opacity: 1, y: 0, rotate: 0)"
                          Stagger="Bm.Stagger(0.03)"
                          Transition="Bm.Spring(bounce: 0.35, duration: 0.7)" />

        @* Words: the same animation, a tenth of the elements. Use this for body copy. *@
        <BmotionSplitText Class="split-headline"
                          Text="One word at a time"
                          By="BmSplitBy.Words"
                          Initial="Bm.To(opacity: 0, y: 20)"
                          Animate="Bm.To(opacity: 1, y: 0)"
                          Stagger="Bm.Stagger(0.08)"
                          Transition="Bm.Spring(bounce: 0.25, duration: 0.6)" />

        @* Lines: splits on the newlines in the string, so the units survive any re-flow. *@
        <BmotionSplitText Class="split-lines"
                          Text="@_stanza"
                          By="BmSplitBy.Lines"
                          Initial='Bm.To(opacity: 0, x: -30, filter: "blur(6px)")'
                          Animate='Bm.To(opacity: 1, x: 0, filter: "blur(0px)")'
                          Stagger="Bm.Stagger(0.12)"
                          Transition="Bm.Tween(0.6, BmEase.Out)" />

        @code {
            private const string _stanza = """
                Split on the newlines
                you actually wrote,
                not on the ones
                the browser happened to pick.
                """;
        }
        """";

    private const string _staggerCode = """
        @using Bit.Bmotion

        @* The stagger is an ordinary Bm.Stagger, so From picks where the cascade begins… *@
        <BmotionSplitText Text="Cascade"
                          Initial="Bm.To(opacity: 0, scale: 0.4, y: 18)"
                          Animate="Bm.To(opacity: 1, scale: 1, y: 0)"
                          Stagger="Bm.Stagger(0.05, from: BmStaggerFrom.Center)"
                          Transition="Bm.Spring(bounce: 0.4, duration: 0.6)" />

        @* …and a custom (index, total) function covers anything the presets do not. *@
        <BmotionSplitText Text="Cascade"
                          Initial="Bm.To(opacity: 0, scale: 0.4, y: 18)"
                          Animate="Bm.To(opacity: 1, scale: 1, y: 0)"
                          Stagger="Bm.Stagger((i, total) => (i % 2 == 0 ? i : total - i) * 0.05)"
                          Transition="Bm.Spring(bounce: 0.4, duration: 0.6)" />
        """;

    private const string _gestureCode = """
        @using Bit.Bmotion

        @* Gesture parameters are forwarded to every unit, so the hover lands on the one
           character the pointer is actually over - no per-letter wiring. *@
        <BmotionSplitText Text="Hover me"
                          WhileHover='Bm.To(y: -14, scale: 1.25, color: "#FD7F36")'
                          Transition="Bm.Spring(stiffness: 400, damping: 18)" />

        @* The reveal-on-scroll headline: no Animate at all, just an in-view overlay that
           plays once. Note Initial is what the text is painted in before it scrolls in. *@
        <BmotionSplitText Text="Reveal on scroll"
                          By="BmSplitBy.Words"
                          Initial="Bm.To(opacity: 0, y: 28)"
                          WhileInView="Bm.To(opacity: 1, y: 0)"
                          Once="true"
                          Stagger="Bm.Stagger(0.08)"
                          Transition="Bm.Spring(bounce: 0.3, duration: 0.7)" />
        """;

    private const string _a11yCode = """
        @using Bit.Bmotion

        @* Renders as:
             <span aria-label="Long copy still wraps…" role="text" style="white-space:pre-wrap">
               <span aria-hidden="true" style="display:inline-block;white-space:nowrap">
                 <span style="display:inline-block">L</span>… one per character
               </span>
               <span> </span>          ← real whitespace, not a unit, so the text wraps here
               …
             </span>

           So: the sentence is announced once, the line breaks fall between words, and a
           selection copies the original string. Set Accessible="false" to opt out when an
           ancestor already labels the text. *@
        <BmotionSplitText Text="Long copy still wraps between words, never inside one."
                          Initial="Bm.To(opacity: 0, y: 10)"
                          Animate="Bm.To(opacity: 1, y: 0)"
                          Stagger="Bm.Stagger(0.012)"
                          Transition="Bm.Tween(0.4)" />
        """;
}
